<h3>Prusa Chamber Light</h3>

<p>
    The on/off button works over USB and needs nothing else.
    <span data-bind="visible: machineType">Detected printer: <code data-bind="text: machineType"></code>.</span>
    <span class="text-warning" data-bind="visible: machineType() && machineType().toUpperCase().indexOf('COREONE') < 0">
        This plugin is only tested on the Prusa CORE One.
    </span>
</p>

<form class="form-horizontal" onsubmit="return false;">
    <div class="control-group">
        <div class="controls">
            <label class="checkbox">
                <input type="checkbox" data-bind="checked: settings.settings.plugins.chamberlight.connect_enabled">
                Brightness control through Prusa Connect (experimental)
            </label>
            <span class="help-block">
                The printer firmware has no G-code for chamber light brightness, so brightness is changed the same way the
                Prusa app does it: through Prusa Connect. This needs internet access and your Prusa account login, and uses an
                <b>unofficial</b> Connect API that Prusa may change or block at any time. Click Save after changing this.
            </span>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div data-bind="visible: settings.settings.plugins.chamberlight.connect_enabled">
        <div class="control-group">
            <label class="control-label">Printer UUID</label>
            <div class="controls">
                <input type="text" class="input-xlarge" data-bind="value: settings.settings.plugins.chamberlight.printer_uuid, attr: { placeholder: firmwareUuid() || 'connect the printer to detect' }">
                <span class="help-block">Leave empty to use the UUID the printer reports over USB<span data-bind="visible: firmwareUuid"> (<code data-bind="text: firmwareUuid"></code>)</span>.
                If Connect says it does not know the printer, open your printer on connect.prusa3d.com and copy the UUID from the address bar, then click Save.</span>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="control-group">
            <label class="control-label">Connect login</label>
            <div class="controls">
                <input type="password" class="input-xlarge" autocomplete="off" data-bind="value: cookieDraft, valueUpdate: 'input'" placeholder="paste auth.refresh_token value">
                <button class="btn" data-bind="click: saveCookie, enable: cookieDraft() && !busy()">Store</button>
                <span class="help-block">
                    Status: <strong data-bind="text: tokenStatus"></strong>
                    <span class="text-error" data-bind="visible: loginError, text: loginError"></span>
                </span>
                <span class="help-block">
                    To get it: log in at connect.prusa3d.com, open the browser's developer tools (F12) &gt; Application &gt;
                    <b>Local storage</b> &gt; https://connect.prusa3d.com, and copy the value of <code>auth.refresh_token</code>.
                    OctoPrint then renews its own login automatically. The token is stored in OctoPrint's config.yaml on this
                    server and is never sent back to the browser. Anyone who can read that file can control your printers
                    through Prusa Connect.
                </span>
                <span class="help-block">
                    Each refresh token can be used only once, so after OctoPrint takes it over, the Connect website in that
                    browser may log you out when it next renews (within about two hours). Log in there again; the website
                    then has its own separate login.
                </span>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="control-group">
            <div class="controls">
                <button class="btn" data-bind="click: sync, enable: cookieSet() && !busy()">Test connection</button>
                <span data-bind="text: syncResult"></span>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</form>
